WEBVTT

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:05.100
Pardon me.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.110
Well, if it isn't Sawaki's other half.

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:10.370
You were still on campus?

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:12.610
Yeah, in those two second-years' dorm.

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:14.440
I see.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:17.220
Sorry to interrupt. We forgot our bags.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:18.920
Sure, they're right there.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:20.560
Thanks.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.200
It must be hard on you, too.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.920
How do you plan to stay 
with Sawaki from now on?

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.560
How...?

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.720
It's not a problem at all.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.490
We're the sons of families who own 
a brewery and a mold-starter business,

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:39.110
so we'll probably always be...

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:40.740
That's not what I meant.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.810
I'll give you a friendly warning.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:48.750
If you consider his 
ability to see microbes

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:50.950
in terms of a complete set

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.020
of transmission electron microscopes,

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:54.650
he's worth 300 million yen.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.780
300 million? No way.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:01.080
If word of his ability got out,

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.900
Professor Itsuki, Whiskers, and Fatso

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.790
wouldn't be the only ones trying 
to take advantage of it.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:44.050
The Microbes Are Gonna Brew!

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:47.890
Wake up. It's morning.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.650
Rhinovirus

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:04.490
S.   cerevisiae

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:04.490
S.   lactis

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:04.490
A.   oryzae

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:02.430
It's pre-chewed sake.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:03.640
Think it's unrefined?

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:04.490
Brew it up!

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:07.090
I'm gonna go check the bulletin board.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:09.650
What's the bulletin board?

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.860
Just part of every 
student's daily routine.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:13.630
I'm not going to class.

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:16.620
I have to, or I'm in trouble.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:19.320
I guess I can go with you.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.050
You should come take a look, too, Sawaki.

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:22.440
Okay.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.050
Huh?

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.600
Kei didn't come back...

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:36.420
I-I only came to get our bags...

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.190
Why did I have to help until morning?

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:40.390
Don't complain.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:43.240
Be grateful that you 
got to see a part of a

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:44.770
graduate student's crucial research.

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:46.700
I just started here yesterday.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.310
I don't understand any of this.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.960
But I guess it did give me a 
chance to study the machinery...

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.670
That's important.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:08.970
Notice: The following students, please report to Professor Itsuki's lab today at 10:00.
First year: Sawaki Tadayasu  
First year: Yuuki Kei  
Second year: Kawahama Takuma  
Second year: Misato Kaoru
You'll regret it if you don't come. -Itsuki

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:05.020
Hey, it's a summons from Itsuki.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:08.970
For you two, it's probably about 
the illicit brewing, right?

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:13.470
So why are Kei and I listed here, too?

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:20.050
Yuuki, it's best to cut ties early 
with useless upperclassmen.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:24.270
Misato and Kawahama 
have no future here.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:26.260
Who has no future?

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:29.620
Oh, the two scumbags and 
Sawaki-kun are here already?

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:33.320
Well, would you leave the 
TEM lab for now, please?

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:34.870
Professor Itsuki will be here soon.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:49.350
Good morning. I see you're all here.

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:51.930
I thought, before we 
start our discussion,

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:55.030
we should celebrate Sawaki-kun 
and Yuuki-kun's first day here.

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:57.230
Bring it in, Oikawa-kun.

00:04:58.130 --> 00:04:59.230
What is that?

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:02.970
She's another new student, 
Oikawa Hazuki-kun.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:05.540
Actually...

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:08.280
I was only passing by...

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.630
Oikawa-kun, would you cut 
that into big pieces for me?

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:12.660
Are you serious?

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:14.290
You've got to be kidding!

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:17.280
Please? I'll give you a credit.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:19.670
Is that... hongeohoe?

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.620
They're bringing out 
something weird again.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:24.590
First a seal, now a stingray?

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:27.830
Hongeohoe is a Korean dish.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:29.340
It's fermented stingray sashimi.

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:32.090
It's fermented?

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:35.100
Weird... there aren't any microbes on it.

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:41.610
Sawaki?!

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:44.280
P. pentosaceus 

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:45.150
P. pentosaceus 

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:47.560
In simple terms, 
the smell of hongeohoe is

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:50.640
five times as strong as that of kiviak,

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:52.530
Hongeohoe

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:50.200
Kiviak

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:52.530
Natto

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.710
and fourteen times that of natto.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:55.580
It's the second strongest smelling 
fermented food in the world.

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:55.580
2nd

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:59.340
In Korea, it's eaten in 
times of celebration.

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:01.460
What do you think, Sawaki-kun? 
Pretty good, isn't it?

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:06.490
On another subject, what exactly 
does your research entail?

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:08.520
Does it have something 
to do with weird stuff

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:10.590
like the seal and this stingray?

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:14.660
Have you heard of terraforming?

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:17.450
It's a theory that originated 
in 1961 as the idea of

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:19.600
modifying planets themselves 
to become habitable.

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:29.150
From a historical point of view,

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:32.130
one could say that the 
Earth is not capable of

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:34.140
flourishing with life for long periods.

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:39.120
However, microorganisms that can 
propagate in harsh environments,

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:43.400
including species that have 
existed since prehistoric times,

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:46.290
present a multitude of possibilities.

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:50.980
So, by studying everything about

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:53.580
living organisms as an agriculturist,

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:56.560
my goal is to come a little 
closer to terraforming.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.020
In other words, Sawaki-kun...

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:04.130
With my knowledge and your 
ability, and if time allows,

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:08.150
it is possible to unlock the 
secrets of that unknown power.

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:09.630
Oh...

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:11.500
...what?

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:12.490
No idea.

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:15.600
Why were we invited to a 
party for the new students?

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:16.240
I dunno.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:19.520
Now then, Hasegawa-kun, if you please.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:20.370
Yes, sir.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:28.780
That room you were illicitly 
brewing sake in

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:31.470
Estimate 
Enclosed

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:31.470
can't be used again 
until it's repaired.

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:34.210
This also includes a bill

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:38.060
for the farming implements that 
were ruined by the sake.

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:40.730
Wh-What are you talking about?

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:44.490
It's in your best interests to just 
take it without any complaints.

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:51.450
10,285,000 yen, after taxes?!

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:53.700
I think it's a reasonable price.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:56.200
So, we'll be counting on you.

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:58.490
Wh-Why are those two being

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:00.720
billed for that much money?

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:04.040
Dunno. We just started at this school.

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:05.610
You reap what you sow.

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:15.780
So you hung out with 
those two yesterday,

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:17.640
and stayed in their dorm overnight?

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:18.600
Yeah.

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:20.490
Want one of these?

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:21.270
What are they?

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:25.270
Antibacterial Wipes

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:23.380
Antibacterial wet wipes.

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:25.270
You should probably wipe your hands.

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:27.280
Antibacterial, huh?

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:29.690
Yet she doesn't mind sitting 
on the ground? Weird girl.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:33.640
Your friend from yesterday 
isn't with you?

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:35.850
Yeah, Kei and the two upperclassmen

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:38.400
were called in by 
Hasegawa-san this morning.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:41.520
Maybe she's making them work 
off that ten million, huh?

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:42.650
I doubt it.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:47.040
Come on, get moving! Whiskers! Fatso!

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:50.670
Student-run Dorm

00:08:57.710 --> 00:09:00.350
Oikawa, you don't have to come with us.

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:02.370
It really is shabby.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:10.290
Never thought I'd see the day when 
a girl would come into this dorm.

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:12.440
Does she know about Sawaki's ability?

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:16.200
Sorry, but we don't have 
time to worry about that.

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:18.940
Eww, it smells so moldy!

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:19.940
See?

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:22.020
Wet wipes won't be 
enough for this place.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:24.500
It's okay. I have spray.

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:27.960
I see...

00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:34.020
Well, come on in.

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:36.090
Might be a little uncomfortable 
for a girl, though.

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:41.890
Don't mind the mess. 
It's as we left it yesterday.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:53.900
I can't take it anymore!

00:09:57.510 --> 00:09:58.770
Thanks...

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:01.050
I can't stand messes!

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:02.410
That's why I told you not to come.

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:06.050
Sawaki, about the 
caterpillar fungus idea.

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:08.010
We want to get started now.

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:10.590
We have to think about 
our debt to Itsuki, too,

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:12.130
but this comes first.

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:15.420
Look. We've put all the money 
we have left into this.

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:17.390
What is it?

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:21.800
Swift moth larvae, to be hosts 
for the caterpillar fungus.

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:24.810
C. sinensis

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:24.810
Putrefying 
Bacteria

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:24.810
If we put the parasites 
on all of these,

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:27.270
Caterpillar Fungus

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:27.270
we can produce lots of 
caterpillar fungus.

00:10:27.950 --> 00:10:31.110
So these are going to turn into 
a valuable herbal medicine...

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:33.480
And how do we cultivate them?

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:37.070
I guess we'll divide up the larvae 
and fungus into petri dishes.

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:39.780
Once we've got more of the fungus,

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:41.240
we'll put them in this—

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:47.630
Oh, it's antibacterial spray.

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:49.410
The moldy smell in here is terrible.

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:52.200
Oh, thanks for doing that.

00:10:52.930 --> 00:10:56.230
His room is like a microbe factory.

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:01.220
Silkworms

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:06.220
C-Caterpillar fungus 
is also a microbe...

00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:11.750
B-Bad news.

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:16.690
You idiot!

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:18.980
Wh-What's your problem now?!

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:21.370
What was the antibacterial 
stuff for?! Damn you!

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:24.030
There are good microbes, too, you know!

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:25.290
What's with you?!

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.520
Sawaki-kun, stop them!

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:30.250
I meant well!

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:33.320
I did it for this filthy room 
and those in it!

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:34.840
You and your antibacterial obsession!

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:36.770
Who cares about cleanliness?!

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:40.200
How many microbes do you think 
are on a single person?!

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:41.410
100 trillion!

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:44.130
If you lined them up, 
they'd encircle the earth five times!

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:45.590
Shut up!

00:11:45.590 --> 00:11:48.390
There are no microbes on me!

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:50.020
Chill out, Oikawa!

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:51.890
And you guys, 
stop taking it out on her!

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:57.900
Mommy!

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:01.410
So you were here.

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:03.990
You shouldn't skip out on 
coming to my lab, Sawaki-kun.

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:09.910
Sugar

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:12.800
Since you didn't come to my lab,

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:15.170
I came here to tell 
you something helpful.

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:18.170
Kind of an unpleasant mood in here...

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:21.010
Because the people living 
here are so irritating.

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:25.140
I hear your caterpillar fungi 
are coming along well.

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:28.380
Things are going well for me, too.

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:30.170
We'll be able to start 
construction right away

00:12:30.170 --> 00:12:32.140
on that room you destroyed.

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:35.140
This is the blueprint for the 
new room I'm having built.

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:36.980
Care for a look, Sawaki-kun?

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:40.550
Blueprint? I don't know how 
to read these things...

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:42.610
Huh?

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:46.110
Hallway

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:46.110
Tank III                      Tank I                         Big Tank

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:46.110
Brewing Room

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:45.360
You're opening up the 
whole first floor.

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:47.610
This isn't so much a room as... 
a brewing cellar?

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:49.140
You thought so, too?

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:49.870
Yeah...

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:51.980
It's not just for sake.

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:53.120
It's a fermenting cellar.

00:12:53.710 --> 00:12:55.810
This will be a lab where we can

00:12:55.810 --> 00:12:58.500
produce and study all 
kinds of fermented foods.

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:00.870
A fermenting cellar?!

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:04.670
So our debt is paying for 
more than just repairs?!

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:07.520
We're not going to pay for 
you to spice up your lab!

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:10.700
Or if we do, you have to 
give us brewing priority!

00:13:16.090 --> 00:13:17.730
What is he trying to do?

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:21.020
Since you two failed at making sake,

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:22.730
I thought I'd teach you a bit.

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:26.400
Hello. I'm Itsuki Keizou.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.450
Today, in celebration of the 
building of the fermenting cellar,

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:33.190
I'd like us to study by 
making unrefined sake.

00:13:33.190 --> 00:13:35.560
That's illicit brewing!

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:36.850
Unrefined sake is often

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:40.040
considered synonymous 
with illicit brewing,

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:42.030
but the university has 
permission to make it

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:44.040
for research purposes, so it's fine.

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:47.130
The basics of fermentation 
and decay are the same:

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:49.860
phenomena that occur through the 
activity of microorganisms.

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:54.530
In layman's terms, when it's useful 
to humans, it's "fermentation,"

00:13:53.390 --> 00:13:56.640
Fermentation

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:56.640
and everything else is 
classified as "decay."

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:56.640
Decay

00:13:57.820 --> 00:13:59.650
Now, let's get to the process.

00:14:00.310 --> 00:14:04.150
First we put rice in a tub and 
add an equal amount of water.

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:08.370
Then we place a rice ball in a 
cloth bag and drop it in, too.

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:10.890
We stir it once a day, 
and after three days,

00:14:10.890 --> 00:14:12.160
it will smell like sake.

00:14:12.900 --> 00:14:16.490
This happens because yeasts in the 
air are cultivated in the water.

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:19.540
We'll save this water to use later.

00:14:19.540 --> 00:14:22.990
Then we steam the rice and 
cool it to body temperature.

00:14:22.990 --> 00:14:25.420
The rice is spread on a straw 
mat and covered with rice malt.

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:29.110
Then the rice is placed in a container 
with the water from before,

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:31.050
covered, and stored in 
a cool, dark place.

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:33.110
You cover it?

00:14:33.110 --> 00:14:35.060
Hm? Do you have a question, Sawaki-kun?

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:39.910
The way you describe it, 
there's only yeast and rice malt in it,

00:14:39.910 --> 00:14:41.690
but there's lots of 
other stuff in here.

00:14:41.690 --> 00:14:44.150
This is just moldy water.

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:47.600
And that brings us to this.

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:49.810
This is the previous mixture, 
after sitting in a room

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:51.660
at 6°C for a few days.

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:57.660
S.   lactis

00:14:54.510 --> 00:14:56.270
We were in the rice ball!

00:14:56.270 --> 00:14:57.660
We can take the cold!

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:02.420
A.  oryzae                  S.  cerevisiae

00:14:57.790 --> 00:14:58.880
It's cold!

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:00.530
So cold, we can't brew!

00:15:00.530 --> 00:15:02.510
The cold wiped out all the other guys.

00:15:03.410 --> 00:15:07.530
And now we gradually 
increase the temperature.

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:12.040
Oh, it's getting warmer.

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:14.040
I feel tired now.

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:16.230
We've made a pretty big mess here.

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:17.430
Let's pull out.

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:21.030
I feel better now that it's warmer!

00:15:21.030 --> 00:15:22.990
I feel much lighter!

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:25.160
Now it's our turn!

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:27.700
This is all ours to brew 
to our hearts' content!

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:28.690
Cool!

00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:31.780
Convert starch to sugar!

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:45.190
Sugar

00:15:31.970 --> 00:15:33.890
Convert starch to sugar!

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:35.870
Convert starch to sugar!

00:15:35.870 --> 00:15:37.420
Convert starch to sugar!

00:15:37.420 --> 00:15:39.000
Wow, that looks tasty!

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:41.750
Convert starch to sugar!

00:15:42.340 --> 00:15:44.900
I ate sugar and alcohol came out!

00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:48.170
In this way, we can make a 
simple brew in just ten days,

00:15:48.170 --> 00:15:51.470
or a more elaborate 
one in about a month.

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:55.090
So, basically, sake is just yeast poop.

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:57.760
In this case, it should 
be called a product of

00:15:57.760 --> 00:15:59.350
fermentation, not poop.

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:02.000
So, the work we've seen the microbes do

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:04.310
with this unrefined sake 
is the basic building block

00:16:04.310 --> 00:16:07.840
of applied microbiology, 
one branch of agricultural science.

00:16:08.390 --> 00:16:12.440
And this is the unrefined 
sake after fermenting.

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:14.010
Hasegawa-kun,

00:16:14.010 --> 00:16:16.030
take a taste, for future reference.

00:16:16.030 --> 00:16:16.620
Okay.

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:20.780
Still, as Hasegawa-kun said earlier,

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:24.250
making sake and vinegar at 
home is against the law,

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:26.940
but it is possible to make 
them at this university,

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:28.500
for research purposes.

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:32.300
That's why there is meaning in 
building a fermenting cellar.

00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:35.370
By making these things with our own...

00:16:36.220 --> 00:16:37.510
Hasegawa-kun?

00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:41.280
Um... it's really sour...

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:43.270
It doesn't even really taste like sake.

00:16:43.270 --> 00:16:45.120
What? That can't be right!

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:48.520
Professor, I don't think 
we should drink this...

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:51.770
It shouldn't taste bad.

00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:55.360
That's my sake.

00:16:55.360 --> 00:16:56.500
What?!

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:59.280
Now that you mention it, 
this is a different container.

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:00.990
When did you switch them?

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:05.750
This is...

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:08.860
I don't use any malt fungi in my sake.

00:17:08.860 --> 00:17:11.630
That's what makes it a real 
sake that anyone can brew.

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:13.670
Take a look!

00:17:13.670 --> 00:17:15.460
This is the rice best 
suited to sake brewing,

00:17:15.460 --> 00:17:17.300
steamed Yamada Nishiki rice!

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:35.760
Pre-chewed sake!

00:17:36.370 --> 00:17:39.520
It uses saliva's ability 
to break down starch.

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:41.830
You chew up and spit out a 
lot of rice, then add water.

00:17:42.340 --> 00:17:43.380
Let's brew!

00:17:43.380 --> 00:17:44.420
What is this?!

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:47.330
S.   cerevisiae

00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:47.400
Then enzymes in the saliva 
mix with the rice

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:49.860
and turn its starch into sugar.

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:51.590
Oh, this rice is sweet.

00:17:51.590 --> 00:17:52.610
Time to brew!

00:17:52.610 --> 00:17:54.340
Hey, it's already turned to sugar.

00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:59.240
A.  oryzae       A.  niger

00:17:54.730 --> 00:17:56.990
Maybe this is fruit, not rice?

00:17:56.990 --> 00:17:59.190
If it's fruit, 
then we're not needed here.

00:17:59.190 --> 00:17:59.750
Let's go.

00:18:00.310 --> 00:18:02.520
We came to build our own houses!

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:10.940
P.   chrysogenum

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:04.000
This sugar is great!

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:06.050
I pooped alcohol...

00:18:06.710 --> 00:18:08.700
Hey, there's alcohol here.

00:18:08.700 --> 00:18:11.850
Aw, man! We can't live in alcohol!

00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:13.610
I pooped alcohol...

00:18:13.990 --> 00:18:16.510
It's the original Japanese sake,

00:18:16.510 --> 00:18:18.120
handed down since ancient times.

00:18:18.120 --> 00:18:19.850
Though, it's supposed to be an

00:18:19.850 --> 00:18:21.960
unmarried woman's job to chew the rice.

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:24.820
Also, I tweaked it a little.

00:18:24.820 --> 00:18:26.280
I added ootheca.

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:27.250
Ootheca?

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:38.470
American                            German
cockroach                         cockroach

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:30.740
It's like a capsule to 
protect insect eggs from

00:18:30.740 --> 00:18:32.860
things like impact and dehydration.

00:18:32.860 --> 00:18:34.450
It's a secret technique

00:18:34.450 --> 00:18:37.370
that's actually known to 
give sake a fuller flavor.

00:18:37.370 --> 00:18:39.760
You always have to butt in...

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:42.140
So there you have it. 
What do you think?

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:43.740
You think I care?!

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:44.930
Don't change the containers!

00:18:44.930 --> 00:18:46.500
What are you trying to make us drink?!

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:48.060
Are you stupid?!

00:18:48.060 --> 00:18:49.170
You call yourself a grad student,

00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:51.060
yet you don't get why 
this sake is so great?!

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:53.470
Pre-chewed sake is miraculous!

00:18:53.470 --> 00:18:55.420
It was made by people 
who had no idea

00:18:55.420 --> 00:18:57.110
what saliva enzymes could do!

00:18:57.510 --> 00:18:59.650
If you're gonna do something, 
you should start from its roots!

00:19:00.260 --> 00:19:02.650
You really are stupid, aren't you?!

00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:05.240
Professor, we'll catch their 
stupidity if we stay here!

00:19:05.240 --> 00:19:06.070
Let's go!

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:10.040
Professor?

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:12.780
I'm so moved...

00:19:14.300 --> 00:19:15.250
Huh?

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:18.890
I have seen the beauty 
of practical science

00:19:18.890 --> 00:19:21.290
stemming from students' own interest.

00:19:24.570 --> 00:19:28.270
You are already true researchers!

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:35.270
I couldn't understand why Sawaki-kun 
voluntarily chose to befriend you two.

00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:38.330
But now it's clear 
that he saw the charm

00:19:38.330 --> 00:19:40.030
in this side of you!

00:19:44.170 --> 00:19:47.470
I'm sorry I gave you such 
a ridiculously high bill.

00:19:47.470 --> 00:19:48.960
Please forgive me.

00:19:48.960 --> 00:19:51.600
Misato-kun and Kawahama-kun,

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:54.500
I want you two to assist me as well!

00:20:00.380 --> 00:20:01.270
Let's brew!

00:20:01.270 --> 00:20:03.250
Let's brew!

00:21:24.920 --> 00:21:27.430
Moyashimon

00:21:24.920 --> 00:21:27.430
Episode 3

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:27.430
Microbe Theater

00:21:26.030 --> 00:21:27.430
Microbe Theater 3!

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:29.820
Hi! I'm A. oryzae!

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:32.740
This time, we're going to introduce 
our fellow malt fungi!

00:21:32.740 --> 00:21:34.650
What are malt fungi, anyway?

00:21:34.650 --> 00:21:36.160
You don't even know that?

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:37.980
How can you not know what you are?

00:21:37.980 --> 00:21:39.380
You philosopher wannabe!

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:42.940
Malt fungi are fungi that 
convert starch to sugar.

00:21:44.570 --> 00:21:45.950
We're A. sojae!

00:21:46.870 --> 00:21:48.630
What do you guys do?

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:49.980
We make miso!

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:51.180
We make soy sauce!

00:21:51.180 --> 00:21:53.660
Because we can live even in salt!

00:21:53.660 --> 00:21:55.320
Wow!

00:21:57.510 --> 00:21:58.510
We're A. niger!

00:21:58.510 --> 00:21:59.880
What do you guys do?

00:21:59.880 --> 00:22:01.390
We make shochu!

00:22:01.390 --> 00:22:03.560
We're also important for making 
black vinegar and citric acid!

00:22:09.450 --> 00:22:11.140
We're A. awamori!

00:22:11.750 --> 00:22:13.430
I bet you make awamori, right?

00:22:14.290 --> 00:22:15.350
C-Correct.

00:22:15.350 --> 00:22:16.500
I guess it was obvious from the name.

00:22:16.500 --> 00:22:18.310
We love warm places!

00:22:18.610 --> 00:22:20.010
We are the malt fungi!

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:22.580
We're gonna do lots of 
brewing from now on!

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:24.900
Let's get brewing!

00:22:24.900 --> 00:22:29.860
In Microbe Theater 
Episode 4,

00:22:24.900 --> 00:22:29.860
We'll Do the Brewing.

